\title{Rational Hilbert Series of Graded $G$-Sets:\\
Transitivity, Finite Differences, and the Order of the Pole at $q=1$}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{August 21, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Let $Y=\bigsqcup_{n\ge0}Y_n$ be a graded $G$-set, i.e.\ a family of sets $Y_n$ each carrying an action of a group $G_n$. For $r\ge0$ let $t_r(Y_n)$ be the number of orbits of $G_n$ on the injective $r$-tuples of $Y_n$, and form the transitivity Hilbert series $H_r(q)=\sum_{n\ge0}t_r(Y_n)q^n$. We prove that if the grades are eventually $r$-transitive then $H_r$ is a rational function whose denominator divides $(1-q)^{r+1}$; in fact the single factor $1-q$ already clears it. The numerator $P$ of the reduced expression satisfies $P(1)=1$, so the pole at $q=1$ is simple with residue $-1$, and when the sub-threshold grades carry no injective $r$-tuple one has exactly $H_r(q)=q^N/(1-q)$. The exponent $r+1$ is optimal in general: for $Y_n=\{1,\dots,n\}$ with the trivial group acting, $t_r(Y_n)=r!\binom{n}{r}$ and $H_r(q)=r!\,q^r/(1-q)^{r+1}$, a pole of order exactly $r+1$; the same graded set with the full symmetric group acting has $H_r(q)=q^r/(1-q)$. The engine is a complete elementary classification: $(1-q)^k\sum_n a_nq^n$ is a polynomial iff the $k$-th forward difference of $a$ vanishes eventually, iff $a$ is eventually a rational combination of the shifted binomials $\binom{n-N}{j}$, $j<k$, iff $a$ is eventually a polynomial of degree $<k$. We also give a transitivity-free route via Burnside's orbit-counting lemma, show that series with poles only at $q=1$ form a subring, and prove that eventual $r$-transitivity makes the entire profile $H_0,\dots,H_r$ rational.
\end{abstract}

